    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p14-23d-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Divide $x^{12}-1$ by $x^3-1$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Quotient: $x^9+x^6+x^3+1$. Remainder: $0$.",
          "worked": "Long division gives successive quotient terms $x^9$, $x^6$, $x^3$ and $1$. The successive subtractions are\n$$\\begin{align*}(x^{12}-1)-x^9(x^3-1)&=x^9-1\\\\(x^9-1)-x^6(x^3-1)&=x^6-1\\\\(x^6-1)-x^3(x^3-1)&=x^3-1\\\\(x^3-1)-(x^3-1)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThus\n$$x^{12}-1=(x^3-1)(x^9+x^6+x^3+1).$$\nThe quotient is $x^9+x^6+x^3+1$ and the remainder is $0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-23d-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that $x^{15}-1$ is divisible by $x^3-1$ and also by $x^5-1$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x^{15}-1=(x^3-1)(x^{12}+x^9+x^6+x^3+1)$ and $x^{15}-1=(x^5-1)(x^{10}+x^5+1)$.\n\nBoth divisions have remainder $0$.",
          "worked": "Dividing by $x^3-1$ gives successive quotient terms $x^{12}$, $x^9$, $x^6$, $x^3$ and $1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}(x^{15}-1)-x^{12}(x^3-1)&=x^{12}-1\\\\(x^{12}-1)-x^9(x^3-1)&=x^9-1\\\\(x^9-1)-x^6(x^3-1)&=x^6-1\\\\(x^6-1)-x^3(x^3-1)&=x^3-1\\\\(x^3-1)-(x^3-1)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nHence\n$$x^{15}-1=(x^3-1)(x^{12}+x^9+x^6+x^3+1).$$\nDividing by $x^5-1$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}(x^{15}-1)-x^{10}(x^5-1)&=x^{10}-1\\\\(x^{10}-1)-x^5(x^5-1)&=x^5-1\\\\(x^5-1)-(x^5-1)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nHence\n$$x^{15}-1=(x^5-1)(x^{10}+x^5+1).$$\nBoth remainders are $0$, so both divisors divide $x^{15}-1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-23d-q6-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Show that $x^{15}-1$ is not divisible by $x^2-1$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Remainder: $x-1$.\n\nThe remainder is nonzero, so $x^{15}-1$ is not divisible by $x^2-1$.",
          "worked": "Long division gives quotient terms $x^{13},x^{11},x^9,x^7,x^5,x^3,x$. Thus\n$$\\begin{align*}x^{15}-1&=(x^2-1)(x^{13}+x^{11}+x^9+x^7+x^5+x^3+x)+(x-1).\\end{align*}$$\nThe remainder $x-1$ has degree $1$, which is less than the divisor's degree $2$. It is not the zero polynomial, so $x^{15}-1$ is not divisible by $x^2-1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 45
      }
    ]
  },
